#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124276 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124469 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0096e314-e53e-5d9b-9cee-fe34fa3a6379" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Vector Form of the Chain Rule to a Function of Two Variables"}] :question/id #uuid "253b765d-be85-574e-8e32-c359ffbecdea" :question/math-academy-id "e-3694" :question/problem "If $f(x,y) = x^2 + 2 y^3$ and $\\mathbf r (t) = \\sin t \\ \\mathbf i + e^{t} \\ \\mathbf j,$ compute $\\dfrac {\\textrm d}{\\textrm d t} [f(\\mathbf r(t))].$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm d}{\\textrm d t} [f(\\mathbf r (t))] = \\nabla f(\\mathbf r(t)) \\cdot \\mathbf r'(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $\\nabla f(x,y){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+2{y}^{3})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+2{y}^{3})\\,j \\\\  & =2x\\,i+6{y}^{2}\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x = \\sin t$ and $y=e^{t}$  into the above to find $\\nabla f(\\mathbf{r}(t)){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =2(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,i+6({e}^{t})^{2}\\,j \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+6{e}^{2t}\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $\\mathbf{r}(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t})\\,j \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+{e}^{t}\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\text{ }i+6{e}^{2t}\\text{ }j)\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\text{ }i+{e}^{t}\\text{ }j) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+6{e}^{2t}\\cdot {e}^{t} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t+6{e}^{3t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124277 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124470 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "112e3378-bde8-5896-822e-e815bde51501" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Vector Form of the Chain Rule to a Function of Three Variables"}] :question/id #uuid "033e459e-5148-5e78-ab5c-7e455b06cd64" :question/math-academy-id "e-3695" :question/problem "If $f(x,y,z) = xz\\ln{y}$ and $\\mathbf r (t) =  t^2 \\, \\mathbf i + t  \\, \\mathbf j +t^3  \\, \\mathbf k,$ compute $\\dfrac {\\textrm d}{\\textrm d t} [f(\\mathbf r(t))].$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm d}{\\textrm d t} [f(\\mathbf r (t))] = \\nabla f(\\mathbf r(t)) \\cdot \\mathbf r'(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $\\nabla f(x,y,z){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(xz\\operatorname{ln}⁡y)\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(xz\\operatorname{ln}⁡y)\\,j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(xz\\operatorname{ln}⁡y)\\,k \\\\  & =z\\operatorname{ln}⁡y\\,i+\\frac{xz}{y}\\,j+x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x= t^2,$ $y = t$ and $z= t^3$ into the above to find $\\nabla f(\\mathbf{r}(t)){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & ={t}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t\\,i+{t}^{4}\\,j+{t}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $\\mathbf{r}(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3})\\,k \\\\  & =2t\\,i+\\,j+3{t}^{2}\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =({t}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t\\,i+{t}^{4}\\,j+{t}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t\\,k)\\cdot (2t\\,i+\\,j+3{t}^{2}\\,k) \\\\  & =({t}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)\\cdot 2t+({t}^{4})\\cdot 1+({t}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)\\cdot 3{t}^{2} \\\\  & =2{t}^{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t+{t}^{4}+3{t}^{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t \\\\  & ={t}^{4}(5\\operatorname{ln}⁡t+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124278 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124471 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ea1e5a55-a957-52cd-b693-0e48770672a6" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Rate of Change of a Multivariable Function at a Point Using the Chain Rule"}] :question/id #uuid "f44b6ea6-694c-5f54-94ee-4c4a699bf4eb" :question/math-academy-id "e-3696" :question/problem "Compute the rate of change of the function $f(x,y,z)=x^2-y^2-z$ with respect to $t$ along the curve $\\mathbf r (t) = \\left \\langle \\sin t, \\cos t, t  \\right \\rangle$ at the point $t=\\dfrac \\pi 4.$" :question/worked-solution "The rate of change of $f(x,y,z)$ with respect to $t$ along the curve $\\mathbf r (t)$ is given by $\\dfrac {\\textrm d}{\\textrm d t} [f(\\mathbf r (t))] .$ To calculate this, we can use the chain rule.\n\nThe chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm d}{\\textrm d t} [f(\\mathbf r (t))] = \\nabla f(\\mathbf{r}(t)) \\cdot \\mathbf{r}'(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $\\nabla f(x,y){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}-{y}^{2}-z),\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}-{y}^{2}-z),\\frac{∂}{∂z}({x}^{2}-{y}^{2}-z)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2x,-2y,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x = \\sin t, y=\\cos t,$ and $z=t$ into the above to find $\\nabla f(\\mathbf r(t)){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =⟨2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $\\mathbf{r}(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =⟨2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,-1⟩\\cdot ⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,1⟩ \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡t-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)-1\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-1 \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the rate of change of $f$ along the curve $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ at $t=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(\\frac{\\pi }{4}))] & =4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})-1 \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}-1 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124404 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124593 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cc017b0d-6418-570a-9a5d-57572e4fe70a" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating the Derivative at a Point Using the Chain Rule"}] :question/id #uuid "c623bb85-401d-5d00-b4a8-cb86570d3304" :question/math-academy-id "e-8282" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\dfrac {\\textrm d}{\\textrm d t}[f(\\mathbf r(t))]$ at $t = \\pi$ given that\n$\\nabla f(x,y) = (2y - 1) \\, \\mathbf i + (2x-1) \\, \\mathbf j$\nand\n$\\mathbf r (t) = \\sin{t} \\, \\mathbf i + \\cos{t} \\, \\mathbf j.$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm d}{\\textrm d t} [f(\\mathbf r (t))] = \\nabla f(\\mathbf r(t)) \\cdot \\mathbf r'(t).\n$$\n\nIn addition, we've been given that\n\n$$\n\\nabla f(x,y) = (2y - 1) \\, \\mathbf i + (2x - 1) \\, \\mathbf j.\n$$\n\nFirst, we substitute $x = \\sin{t}$ and $y = \\cos{t}$ into $\\nabla f(x,y)$ to find $\\nabla f(\\mathbf{r}(t)){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-1)\\,i+(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-1)\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $\\mathbf{r}(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,j \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-1\\,i+(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-1)\\,j)\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,j) \\\\  & =(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-1)\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡t+(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-1)\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =2{cos}^{2}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-2{sin}^{2}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\  & =2({cos}^{2}⁡t-{sin}^{2}⁡t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the above at $t = \\pi,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(\\pi ))] & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\pi +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi  \\\\  & =2(1)+0-(-1) \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445679 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124470 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "112e3378-bde8-5896-822e-e815bde51501" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Vector Form of the Chain Rule to a Function of Three Variables"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3a36a033-d9b0-5aa7-a7f4-b5451390ac7a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445648} {:answer/id #uuid "60dab585-b4f4-59ea-8d98-9a5182b158eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445654} {:answer/id #uuid "8d6d49e7-278c-5501-995f-670dd3f19484" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2+2t)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445660} {:answer/id #uuid "d82ae990-52e1-5862-b9ec-b3ce7c4bd555" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445667} {:answer/id #uuid "ff848b94-7fca-53f6-95e5-3ab48c4f01c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{1}{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445672}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d82ae990-52e1-5862-b9ec-b3ce7c4bd555" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445667} :answer-field/id #uuid "88e49b12-6312-563d-bb5a-a9112af44abf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445674}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "430b6986-f30c-500a-b5e9-219dd0f3ace5" :question/math-academy-id "q-167463" :question/problem "If $f(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xyz)$ and $r(t)=t\\,i+2t\\,j+\\frac{1}{t}\\,k,$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))].$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xyz))\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xyz))\\,j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xyz))\\,k \\\\  & =yz\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xyz)\\,i+xz\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xyz)\\,j+xy\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xyz)\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x=t,$ $y=2t$ and $z=\\frac{1}{t}$ into the above to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\,j+2{t}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t)\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{t})\\,k \\\\  & =\\,i+2\\,j-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\,j+2{t}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\,k)\\cdot (\\,i+2\\,j-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}\\,k) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\cdot 1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\cdot 2+2{t}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124959 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124470 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "112e3378-bde8-5896-822e-e815bde51501" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Vector Form of the Chain Rule to a Function of Three Variables"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2d6b5248-8092-5223-a861-76c990d98151" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{t+1}+(1+2t){e}^{t}+e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445647} {:answer/id #uuid "3f8c2a6a-498f-5431-bf0a-f09364aeca5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{e}^{t}+2e+t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445649} {:answer/id #uuid "55795f5b-54c2-5a78-8f3f-d7bc330a0c55" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{t+1}+(t+1){e}^{t}+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445653} {:answer/id #uuid "88d607c8-fe3c-5a8f-95e2-26b4560aaab5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{t+1}+(1+t){e}^{t}+e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445659} {:answer/id #uuid "9a2dcd24-b4f5-5abd-afe6-bb8c91b06230" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{t+1}+t{e}^{t}+e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445663}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "88d607c8-fe3c-5a8f-95e2-26b4560aaab5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{t+1}+(1+t){e}^{t}+e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445659} :answer-field/id #uuid "00f9b55f-9f7a-5171-8a0f-7462d539b472" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122354}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f10e177e-2926-5cde-8e9a-4b0a99b2ff4c" :question/math-academy-id "q-36238" :question/problem "If $f(x,y,z)=xy+yz+xz$ and $r(t)=t\\,i+{e}^{t}\\,j+e\\,k,$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))].$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(xy+yz+xz)\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(xy+yz+xz)\\,j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(xy+yz+xz)\\,k \\\\  & =(y+z)\\,i+(x+z)\\,j+(y+x)\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x=t,y={e}^{t},$ and $z=e$ into the above to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =({e}^{t}+e)\\,i+(t+e)\\,j+({e}^{t}+t)\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t})\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(e)\\,k \\\\  & =i+{e}^{t}\\,j+(0)\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =(({e}^{t}+e)\\,i+(t+e)\\,j+({e}^{t}+t)\\,k)\\cdot (i+{e}^{t}\\,j+(0)\\,k) \\\\  & =({e}^{t}+e)\\cdot 1+(t+e)\\cdot {e}^{t}+({e}^{t}+t)\\cdot 0 \\\\  & ={e}^{t}+e+t{e}^{t}+{e}^{t+1} \\\\  & ={e}^{t+1}+(1+t){e}^{t}+e.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124960 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124471 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ea1e5a55-a957-52cd-b693-0e48770672a6" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Rate of Change of a Multivariable Function at a Point Using the Chain Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bde96964-49e5-5f8c-9a85-bf83f3f624a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-17" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122361} {:answer/id #uuid "c18873f1-6416-5e3f-84a8-30f0d1e34a57" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122362} {:answer/id #uuid "eb8b5412-a282-5231-a52a-c430c381a304" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122363} {:answer/id #uuid "55bd4f06-001b-5b40-9449-067e29d3fd21" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122364} {:answer/id #uuid "5c13ba27-5c90-535c-9ea9-9232444e86bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122365}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bde96964-49e5-5f8c-9a85-bf83f3f624a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-17" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122361} :answer-field/id #uuid "30ce640c-974a-53b3-8a92-6e2b4d383ffe" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122360}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e234e1ce-e3a5-5460-9b85-a8876cb6e36c" :question/math-academy-id "q-36247" :question/problem "Compute the rate of change of the function $f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}+3xyz$ with respect to $t$ along the curve $r(t)=⟨{t}^{2},t+1,-t⟩$ at the point $t=1.$" :question/worked-solution "The rate of change of $f(x,y,z)$ with respect to $t$ along the curve $r(t)$ is given by $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))].$ To calculate this, we can use the chain rule.\n\nThe chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+3xyz),\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+3xyz),\\frac{∂}{∂z}({x}^{2}+3xyz)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2x+3yz,3xz,3xy⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x={t}^{2},y=t+1,$ and $z=-t$ into the above to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =⟨2({t}^{2})+3(t+1)(-t),3({t}^{2})(-t),3({t}^{2})(t+1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2{t}^{2}-3{t}^{2}-3t,-3{t}^{3},3{t}^{3}+3{t}^{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-{t}^{2}-3t,-3{t}^{3},3{t}^{3}+3{t}^{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t+1),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2t,1,-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =⟨-{t}^{2}-3t,-3{t}^{3},3{t}^{3}+3{t}^{2}⟩\\cdot ⟨2t,1,-1⟩ \\\\  & =(-{t}^{2}-3t)\\cdot 2t+(-3{t}^{3})\\cdot 1+(3{t}^{3}+3{t}^{2})\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =-2{t}^{3}-6{t}^{2}-3{t}^{3}-3{t}^{3}-3{t}^{2} \\\\  & =-8{t}^{3}-9{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the rate of change of $f$ along the curve $r(t)$ at $t=1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(1))] & =-8{(1)}^{3}-9{(1)}^{2}=-17.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124961 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124471 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ea1e5a55-a957-52cd-b693-0e48770672a6" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Rate of Change of a Multivariable Function at a Point Using the Chain Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ace15edc-01db-5886-b390-82e1ee604ab0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\cos 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122367} {:answer/id #uuid "b8fa671d-44d0-5673-bcc2-5869484b719b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3\\cos 8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122368} {:answer/id #uuid "0b632d6e-7a98-53fb-a62b-8c9001dbea44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122369} {:answer/id #uuid "256c9cc6-616a-5cb0-87f5-11a7534c3ee6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3\\cos 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122370} {:answer/id #uuid "621b4a41-2040-513d-8f05-896248a7bf71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\cos 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122371}] :answer-field/id #uuid "bbe9c851-9ada-5f1b-ae5b-56517e51d11b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122366}] :question/id #uuid "8571bcaa-6cdf-5385-98c4-d1ed2624cfd7" :question/math-academy-id "q-36253" :question/problem "Compute the rate of change of the function $f(x, y) = \\sin (x - y)$ with respect to $t$ along the curve $r(t) = ⟨2\\sqrt{t}, 4t⟩$ at the point $t = 1$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445677 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124593 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cc017b0d-6418-570a-9a5d-57572e4fe70a" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating the Derivative at a Point Using the Chain Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05ab9250-28af-5908-8d6f-09833d521f51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445643} {:answer/id #uuid "67d5968e-4c9e-5d9b-9b55-ddaa5793f241" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445655} {:answer/id #uuid "bd8e760c-2dd0-5dcf-8836-3c5e2deced71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445664} {:answer/id #uuid "cc6cc197-cc16-54f4-b4ca-9fa9bd9e20c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445666} {:answer/id #uuid "de9daefe-3ccb-54d5-9516-f29765d78d78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445668}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "67d5968e-4c9e-5d9b-9b55-ddaa5793f241" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445655} :answer-field/id #uuid "da5c7c2a-000b-5483-8ada-2cf63e301ba8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445675}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "021b7b63-3816-5a7a-9f77-baf36bf193bb" :question/math-academy-id "q-65864" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]$ at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ given that $∇f(x,y)=\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,i+\\frac{2y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,j$ and $r(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\text{ }i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\text{ }j.$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nIn addition, we've been given that\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,i+\\frac{2y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,j.\n$$\n\nFirst, we substitute $x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t$ and $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$ into $∇f(x,y)$ to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{{sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t}\\,i+\\frac{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{{sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t}\\,j \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,j \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j)\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,j) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡t+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the above at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(\\frac{\\pi }{4}))] & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125246 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124593 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cc017b0d-6418-570a-9a5d-57572e4fe70a" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating the Derivative at a Point Using the Chain Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15d810bf-905d-5d78-ba48-12ddc9b892e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2-\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357945} {:answer/id #uuid "2a6ad6c1-0c8d-5fb0-b435-dee857f73211" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358135} {:answer/id #uuid "7ae9204f-c34d-510e-94bc-023fcf5f25fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358819} {:answer/id #uuid "9af214c2-c6da-5e82-8df8-28a9a5b98d27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359108} {:answer/id #uuid "acbb10f0-bfb6-5636-9744-b6306c87e6ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359243}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9af214c2-c6da-5e82-8df8-28a9a5b98d27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359108} :answer-field/id #uuid "b1da5512-5fc1-5bb7-8ab0-03b3b2b0ba70" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360309}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "72c15c65-cd14-5a6c-9b4a-a63cd2f705de" :question/math-academy-id "q-94872" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]$ at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ given that $∇f(x,y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,i+x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,j$ and $r(t)=\\frac{t}{2}\\,i+t\\,j.$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nIn addition, we've been given that\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,i+x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,j.\n$$\n\nFirst, we substitute $x=\\frac{t}{2}$ and $y=t$ into $∇f(x,y)$ to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\frac{t}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{t}{2})\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\,i+j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\frac{t}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j)\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2}\\,i+j) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{t}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the above at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(\\frac{\\pi }{2}))] & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(1+\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\cdot 0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445678 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124469 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0096e314-e53e-5d9b-9cee-fe34fa3a6379" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Vector Form of the Chain Rule to a Function of Two Variables"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c059460-c4c2-55a2-8585-7e51d8edae78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{sin}^{2}⁡(2t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445644} {:answer/id #uuid "47522fd9-62bd-5474-804d-67ac7a770b4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{sin}^{4}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445650} {:answer/id #uuid "4b40ea9e-cf34-5238-9134-0581b982e962" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445651} {:answer/id #uuid "c7da010b-a10a-5868-8d20-e4e46c3b4c9d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445665} {:answer/id #uuid "e85749fe-f788-582a-a8ba-6a6fb947faa2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{cos}^{3}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445669}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c7da010b-a10a-5868-8d20-e4e46c3b4c9d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445665} :answer-field/id #uuid "47babdd0-5f4c-5c9a-9ce1-4d934697545f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445673}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "240fc49a-d022-5d2c-b6ad-58058500b98b" :question/math-academy-id "q-95051" :question/problem "If $f(x,y)={x}^{4}-{y}^{4}$ and $r(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j,$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))].$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{4}-{y}^{4})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{4}-{y}^{4})\\,j \\\\  & =4{x}^{3}\\,i-4{y}^{3}\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$ and $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t$ into the above to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =4{cos}^{3}⁡t\\,i-4{sin}^{3}⁡t\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,j \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =(4{cos}^{3}⁡t\\,i-4{sin}^{3}⁡t\\,j)\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j) \\\\  & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{cos}^{3}⁡t-4{sin}^{3}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\  & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t({cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t) \\\\  & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125250 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124470 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "112e3378-bde8-5896-822e-e815bde51501" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Vector Form of the Chain Rule to a Function of Three Variables"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "98f9b817-55fe-5337-9917-12a57b7f32fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2e^{t}\\sin t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186124101} {:answer/id #uuid "0ae1149c-5a8d-577c-9d59-3c421a3478e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2e^{t} + \\sin t + \\cos t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186124102} {:answer/id #uuid "0f80eba8-e277-54b6-a822-397fce4bd264" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186124103} {:answer/id #uuid "1f972039-9ac7-5dd8-94b0-eea94f14f776" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2e^{t}(\\sin t + \\cos t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186124104} {:answer/id #uuid "432cd37d-71ad-5244-a79d-53312dec9e11" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2e^{t}\\cos t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186124105}] :answer-field/id #uuid "f6d53ae6-99b0-5b9a-984f-d8cbdd57249f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186124100}] :question/id #uuid "9127ceaf-3e13-50a4-b08a-89114324036d" :question/math-academy-id "q-95075" :question/problem "If $f(x, y, z) = z(x + y)$ and $r(t) = \\sin ti + \\cos tj + e^{t}k$, compute $\\frac{d}{dt}[f(r(t))]$."}]}
